---
layout: layouts/base.njk
title: International comparison
description: How UK government debt compares with other major economies, using a consistent international measure.
eleventyComputed:
  dataUpdated: "{{ [internationalComparisons.retrieved_date, budget2026.lastUpdated] | latestOf }}"
---
{% from "components/macros.njk" import caveatBox, dataFreshnessBadge %}

<div class="page-intro prose">
  <h1>International comparison</h1>
  <p class="lede">How the UK sits against other major economies, on a measure chosen to be comparable across countries.</p>
</div>

{{ caveatBox(internationalComparisons.caveat, "Comparing like with like") }}

<section aria-labelledby="table-heading">
  <h2 id="table-heading">{{ internationalComparisons.measure }}, {{ internationalComparisons.year }}</h2>
  <div class="table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="International debt comparison table">
    <table class="data-table">
      <caption>Sorted from highest to lowest debt-to-GDP. All figures are for {{ internationalComparisons.year }}, the latest completed year.</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Country</th>
          <th scope="col" class="num">Debt %GDP</th>
          <th scope="col" class="num">Deficit %GDP</th>
          <th scope="col" class="num">Growth %</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {%- for c in internationalComparisons.countries | sort(true, false, "debtToGdp") %}
        <tr{% if c.id == "uk" %} class="is-highlight"{% endif %}>
          <th scope="row">{{ c.name }}{% if c.id == "uk" %} <span class="tag">UK</span>{% endif %}</th>
          <td class="num">{{ c.debtToGdp }}</td>
          <td class="num">{{ c.deficitToGdp }}</td>
          <td class="num">{{ c.gdpGrowth }}</td>
        </tr>
        {%- endfor %}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</section>

<p class="source-note">
  Debt figures: <a href="{{ internationalComparisons.source_url }}" rel="noopener">{{ internationalComparisons.source_name }}</a>, {{ internationalComparisons.year }}.
  Retrieved {{ internationalComparisons.retrieved_date | timeTag | safe }}.
  {{ dataFreshnessBadge(internationalComparisons.confidence_level, "") }}
</p>

<section class="prose" aria-labelledby="note-heading">
  <h2 id="note-heading">Why the UK number here differs from the headline</h2>
  <p>This table uses <strong>general government gross debt</strong>, the most consistent measure across countries. The UK's own headline measure (public sector net debt, around {{ (dashboard.metrics | findBy("id", "debt-gdp")).value | round }}% of GDP) is lower because it subtracts liquid assets and is defined slightly differently. Comparing countries fairly means using the same definition for all of them, even if it is not the one a country usually quotes at home.</p>
</section>

{% set eu = budget2026.euLimits %}
{% set de = internationalComparisons.countries | findBy("id", "de") %}
{% set fr = internationalComparisons.countries | findBy("id", "fr") %}
{% set it = internationalComparisons.countries | findBy("id", "it") %}
<div class="prose">
  <h2 id="eu-limits">The UK's figures on the EU's measures</h2>
  <p>The EU's fiscal rules, the Stability and Growth Pact, ask member states to keep the government deficit under <strong>{{ eu.deficitLimitPct }}% of GDP</strong> and gross government debt under <strong>{{ eu.debtLimitPct }}% of GDP</strong>. Countries joining the euro are judged against the same two values. Joining the EU itself is a different test: the accession criteria ask for a functioning market economy, and set no deficit or debt figure.</p>
  {%- set firstUnder = budget2026.path.years | findBy("year", eu.uk.firstYearUnderDeficitLimit) %}
  <p>The OBR publishes UK figures on the EU's measures, by financial year. The deficit (general government net borrowing) is {{ (budget2026.path.years | findBy("year", "2026-27")).ggnbPct }}% of GDP in 2026-27 and first falls under {{ eu.deficitLimitPct }}% in {{ firstUnder.year }}, at {{ firstUnder.ggnbPct }}%. Gross debt stays between {{ eu.uk.grossDebtMinPct | round }}% and {{ eu.uk.grossDebtMaxPct | round }}% of GDP in every year of the forecast, so the UK would miss the debt limit throughout.</p>
  <div class="table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="UK figures on the EU measures, by year">
    <table class="data-table data-table--years">
      <caption>UK on the EU's measures, % of GDP, financial years. The limits are {{ eu.deficitLimitPct }} for the deficit and {{ eu.debtLimitPct }} for debt. 2025-26 is outturn, still provisional. Later years are OBR forecasts.</caption>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Year</th><th scope="col" class="num">Deficit</th><th scope="col" class="num">Gross debt</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {%- for y in budget2026.path.years %}
        <tr><th scope="row">{{ y.year }}</th><td class="num">{{ y.ggnbPct | oneDp }}</td><td class="num">{{ y.ggDebtPct | oneDp }}</td></tr>
        {%- endfor %}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>In the country table at the top of this page, the three EU members shown are all over the debt limit: Germany at {{ de.debtToGdp }}%, France at {{ fr.debtToGdp }}% and Italy at {{ it.debtToGdp }}%. France ({{ fr.deficitToGdp }}%) and Italy ({{ it.deficitToGdp }}%) are also over the deficit limit. Those figures are the IMF's, for calendar year {{ internationalComparisons.year }}. The OBR's are by financial year, so the two do not match exactly: the UK row in the country table reads {{ (internationalComparisons.countries | findBy("id", "uk")).debtToGdp }}%, while the OBR's 2025-26 figure is {{ (budget2026.path.years | findBy("year", "2025-26")).ggDebtPct }}%. The caveat at the top of this page explains the difference.</p>
  <p class="source-note">
    Limits: <a href="{{ eu.limits_source_url }}" rel="noopener">{{ eu.limits_source_name }}</a>.
    Euro criteria: <a href="{{ eu.euro_source_url }}" rel="noopener">{{ eu.euro_source_name }}</a>.
    Accession criteria: <a href="{{ eu.accession_source_url }}" rel="noopener">{{ eu.accession_source_name }}</a>.
    Retrieved {{ eu.retrieved_date | timeTag | safe }}. {{ dataFreshnessBadge(eu.confidence_level, "") }}
    UK figures: <a href="{{ budget2026.path.source_url }}" rel="noopener">{{ budget2026.path.source_name }}</a>.
    Retrieved {{ budget2026.path.retrieved_date | timeTag | safe }}. {{ dataFreshnessBadge(budget2026.path.confidence_level, "") }}
  </p>
</div>
